{"title":"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A game of solitaire is played with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> red cards, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> white cards, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> blue cards. A player plays all the cards one at a time. With each play he accumulates a penalty. If he plays a blue card, then he is charged a penalty which is the number of white cards still in his hand. If he plays a white card, then he is charged a penalty which is twice the number of red cards still in his hand. If he plays a red card, then he is charged a penalty which is three times the number of blue cards still in his hand. Find, as a function of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/1/d01c63cc080de93f1d63563a02d1f2cb6a1f2c68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R, W,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/ccedc2f7ab3f66b36d3e0cf1536a3c801f01f421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> the minimal total penalty a player can amass and all the ways in which this minimum can be achieved.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We claim (inductively) that the minimum is just going to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3173d5fdba7da8c431904364b6f46a280bb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(BW,2WR,3RB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"171\" height=\"18\" />. We'll start our induction with the case where one of the three quantities is zero, in which case we verify that we can indeed get away without any penalty by, for example, discarding blue if we are out of white.\n</p><p>Now, for the inductive step, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/e/f2eb179c5275c86ed81280076604389a47dd4f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(B,W,R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> be the minimum we seek. Note that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/2/4523db546732d30923c6125b3a88362093eca105.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(B,W,R) = \\min(W+f(B-1,W,R),2R+f(B,W-1,R),3B+f(B,W,R-1))\\]\" width=\"610\" height=\"18\" />\nBy our inductive hypothesis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/3/6235cbfd53b65f45c95fcf14d597bc9c6d5ca4bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(B-1,W,R) = \\min((B-1)W,2WR,3R(B-1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"403\" height=\"18\" />. In order for this to cause our inductive step not to hold, we would require that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/8470472bceb91598aaae26d58dd27d4799f0e981.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+\\min((B-1)W,2WR,3R(B-1)) &lt; \\min(BW,2WR,3RB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"504\" height=\"20\" />. It is evident that the first two entries in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/958563059042b1cc80498ccde6f26d9d07bf6c02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$min$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> expression cannot cause this to happen, so that we need only consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/c/8ecfd29736d4a82140a2c2904b2d38c5ea4a998b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+3R(B-1) &lt; \\min(BW,2WR,3RB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"320\" height=\"18\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/7/267c915af98213f8adf279c6f124d6c6e29bc209.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+3R(B-1) &lt; BW$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"183\" height=\"18\" />, whence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/6/4b656068728e39cbf1d7b00dc745142b7e6c5639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3R &lt; W$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"67\" height=\"13\" />. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/2/f9229819944495d8bf322f9b440dc89032b99000.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+3R(B-1) &lt; 3RB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"186\" height=\"18\" />, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46b570fcfe9578395649d5592980f617e7ddeef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W &lt; 3R$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"67\" height=\"13\" />, a contradiction.\n</p><p>For the other two cases, we can get similar contradictions, so that our inductive step must hold, and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/e/f2eb179c5275c86ed81280076604389a47dd4f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(B,W,R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> is indeed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3173d5fdba7da8c431904364b6f46a280bb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(BW,2WR,3RB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"171\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>We now need only establish how many ways to do this. If one of these quantities is smaller, our induction and the fact that it is eventually zero guarantees that it will continue to be the smallest quantity as cards are discarded. (For example, if it is currently optimal to discard a blue card, it will continue to be so until we run out of blue cards.) Therefore, assuming that there is currently only one best choice of card to discard, this will continue to be so in the future, whence if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/9/02964a99b7bf9c6e318231249e4e88d735b3b78e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW \\neq 2WR \\neq 3RB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"162\" height=\"17\" />, there is only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> optimal strategy.\n</p><p>Suppose, now, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/7/a0731df4883c87551bc95726bf50272a04704fce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" />. It is thus optimal to discard either a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> card. If we ever discard a blue card, then we will cause <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/0/900866224f38a63f0c19e5e5d974dcc1568189e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW &lt; 2WR$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"101\" height=\"13\" />, whence there is only one possible strategy from then on. However, if we discard a white card, then we will still have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/7/a0731df4883c87551bc95726bf50272a04704fce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" />, meaning that we continue to have the choice of discarding a white or blue card. Since we can discard a white card at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> times, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c995aa5d4d8e746c09f42ca6f4b90c63cb25663c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> choices for how many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> cards to discard (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" />), meaning that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c995aa5d4d8e746c09f42ca6f4b90c63cb25663c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> optimal strategies.\n</p><p>By similar logic, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/4/ec44f1958c6ae29742d9cea156c3996921acad42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> optimal strategies if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/d/04dededfd3f55103a196ed23154c03d6f9835948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2WR = 3RB$\" width=\"104\" height=\"12\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/6/d26e969cea5b7b222093de9bd5ffd05bb3061a65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> optimal strategies if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27ff5e6e35662f37ab5977c7eaa623196219766a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3RB = BW$\" width=\"96\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>The final case, then, is if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/1/691ac6a0151add1e85c47a4330084f80406dd700.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR = 3RB$\" width=\"162\" height=\"12\" />. In this case, if we first discard a white card, we are left with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/7/a0731df4883c87551bc95726bf50272a04704fce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> case, and similarly for a blue and red card. The total number of optimal strategies in this case is then the sum of the optimal strategies in those cases, or, in other words, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/a/4da1a0bd1827c34c35d803e9f555c87a6651e982.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B+W+R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>To summarize:\nThe minimum penalty is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3173d5fdba7da8c431904364b6f46a280bb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(BW,2WR,3RB)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"171\" height=\"18\" />.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/9/02964a99b7bf9c6e318231249e4e88d735b3b78e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW \\neq 2WR \\neq 3RB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"162\" height=\"17\" />, there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> optimal strategy.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/e/29ecf0c6b91a87e39ee84ed5c9d4dacf14938ad8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR &lt; 3RB$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"162\" height=\"13\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c995aa5d4d8e746c09f42ca6f4b90c63cb25663c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> strategies.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/c/66c63ee5c2e3bfc743de2b84cf9944ef6826fa8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2WR = 3RB &lt; BW$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"163\" height=\"13\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/4/ec44f1958c6ae29742d9cea156c3996921acad42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> strategies.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/4/ef4cf58e3768eb0ac3ccc030bb2ef4f59bc39fe2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3RB = BW &lt; 2WR$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"163\" height=\"13\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/6/d26e969cea5b7b222093de9bd5ffd05bb3061a65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> strategies.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/1/691ac6a0151add1e85c47a4330084f80406dd700.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BW = 2WR = 3RB$\" width=\"162\" height=\"12\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/2/4529fd99c98f55c3fb7fa2e39a0aa86e4f1ad879.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R+B+W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"92\" height=\"14\" /> strategies.\n</p><p>By J Steinhardt, from AoPS Community\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO\" title=\"2000 USAMO\">2000 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2000\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2000 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017012913\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.031 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.051 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 288/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1313/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8698/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.449      1 -total\n 58.03%    7.224      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n 40.30%    5.017      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:11042:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017012913 and revision id 109468. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}